%N A398340 Triangle read by rows, where T(n,k), n>0, k<=2^(n-1), is the smallest prime factor of (2*k-1)*2^n+1.
%C A398340 For n >= 1, T(n,k) > 2 since (2*k-1)*2^n+1 is odd. T(n,k) = 3 if n is odd and k == 1 mod 3. T(n,k) = 3 if n is even and k == 0 mod 3. T(n,k) > 3 otherwise. This implies that a(n) = 3 if and only if n == 1 mod 3. Proof: if n is odd and k == 1 mod 3, then 2^n == 2 mod 3 and 2k-1 == 1 mod 3, thus (2*k-1)*2^n+1 is a multiple of 3. Similarly, if n is even and k == 0 mod 3, then 2^n == 1 mod 3 and 2k-1 == 2 mod 3 and (2*k-1)*2^n+1 is a multiple of 3. For the rest of the cases, (2*k-1)*2^n+1 is not a multiple of 3  - _Chai Wah Wu_, Sep 16 2026
%H A398340 Chai Wah Wu, <a href="/A398340/b398340.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A398340 a(n) = 3 if and only if n == 1 mod 3. - _Chai Wah Wu_, Sep 16 2026
%e A398340 The least prime factor of (2*1-1)*2^1 + 1 = 3 is 3. So T(1,1) = 3.
%e A398340 First rows are:
%e A398340   3;
%e A398340   5, 13;
%e A398340   3, 5, 41, 3;
%e A398340   17, 7, 3, 113, 5, 3, 11, 241;
%e A398340   3, 97, 7, 3, 17, 353, 3, 13, 5, 3, 673, 11, 3, 5, 929, 3;
%e A398340   ...
%t A398340 A398340[n_, k_] := FactorInteger[(2*k - 1)*2^n + 1][[1, 1]];
%t A398340 Table[A398340[n, k], {n, 7}, {k, 2^(n - 1)}] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 17 2026 *)
%o A398340 (Python)
%o A398340 from itertools import count
%o A398340 def A398340_T(n,k): return next(filter(lambda p:pow(2,n,p)*((k<<1)-1)%p==p-1,count(3,2))) # _Chai Wah Wu_, Sep 16 2026
